    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-question">When is the cheapest time to visit Florida's Gulf Coast?</h3>
        <p class="faq-answer">Fall (September&ndash;November) offers the lowest rates at $499/night average &mdash; nearly $190/night less than the spring peak of $686/night, a 27% savings. Weather stays warm (80&ndash;86&deg;F), Gulf water temperature holds in the low 80s through November, and crowds are minimal. A fall trip saves $935 on lodging alone over 5 nights compared to spring.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-question">What are the best ways to save money on a Gulf Coast Florida vacation?</h3>
        <p class="faq-answer">The biggest savings: (1) Stay in Bradenton instead of AMI or Sarasota and save $460&ndash;$2,675 on lodging alone. (2) Cook breakfast and lunch at the rental to save $800+ over 5 nights. (3) Book directly with the property manager to avoid 10&ndash;15% Airbnb service fees ($500 savings on a typical spring booking). (4) Shop at Detwiler's Farm Market or Aldi instead of Publix (20&ndash;30% cheaper). (5) Use free attractions: beach parking, AMI trolley, De Soto National Memorial, Robinson Preserve.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-question">How much does it cost to eat out in Bradenton and Sarasota?</h3>
        <p class="faq-answer">Bradenton mid-range dinner for two: $50&ndash;$70 (Pier 22, Star Fish Company). Sarasota mid-range dinner for two: $70&ndash;$100 (Owen's Fish Camp, Columbia Restaurant). Anna Maria Island mid-range dinner for two: $80&ndash;$120 (The Sandbar, Beach Bistro). Budget options: Anna Maria Oyster Bar ($15&ndash;$25/person), Duffy's Tavern ($1 beers on AMI). Domestic beer everywhere: $5&ndash;$8. Cocktails: $12&ndash;$20.</p>
    </div>
</div>


